Try your best to implement a generic class called Pair that represents
a pair of things.

Specifically, a pair is simply a collection of two objects. Those two things
may be of different types.

For example, I might want to construct a pair that consists of an integer
and a string.

Or maybe I want to construct a pair that consists of a Rocket and a Pokemon.

Hint: Your Pair generic class will need TWO generic type parameters.
It must have two fields, and a constructor that accepts two corresponding
arguments to initialize those fields. The fields should be public.
They can have whatever names you want.

class Pair[T1, T2]:
	field1: T1
	field2: T2

	def __init__(self, arg1: T1, arg2: T2) -> None:
		self.field1 = arg1
		self.field2 = arg2

	

